Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Spike 3: the 64-bit firmware boots Linux

Re-ports the bzImage loader to arch/amd64 and gives it a 64-bit handoff. The
checkpoint is unchanged: showcase-rival-boots-linux.sh amd64 reaches
"Welcome to u-root!".

The port itself is mechanical -- arch/x86/linux_load.c is a strict superset of
the dead amd64 fork (which still #includes the deleted loadfs.h), so the x86
file is the base and the amd64 delta is re-applied on top. Taking the old fork
as the base would have silently dropped two revival-patch fixes it predates.

What was NOT mechanical is that arch/amd64 does not relocate itself. Long mode
ignores segment bases, so Spike 1 dropped relocate() -- correctly -- and the
consequence nobody wrote down is that the firmware permanently occupies the
1 MiB a bzImage runs at, while arch/x86/linux_load.c assumes throughout that
the firmware has moved out of the way. Three of the five defects below are that
assumption showing through, and every one of the five failed SILENTLY.

1. config/examples/amd64_config.xml had CONFIG_FSYS_ISO9660 = false where x86
   has true, so the loader never saw a file. (FSYS_FAT and FSYS_UFS still
   differ; only ISO9660 is flipped here, because only it was measured.)

2. The kernel was read straight to 0x100000, over the running firmware. Not a
   crash: the machine printed "Loading kernel... " and never the "ok", because
   overwriting your own text raises no fault -- so Spike 2's exception handler,
   which had diagnosed every previous 64-bit bug, had nothing to say. The
   kernel is now staged above _end and a position-independent stub parked at
   0x80000 copies it down over the firmware and lretq's to kern_addr + 0x200.

3. That stub then demolished the page tables it was being translated through:
   pml4 is in .bss at 0x184000, inside the destination. Writing from 0x100000
   it reaches them after 0x84000 bytes, while reading from 0x284000; QEMU's
   -d int reported #PF at CR2=0x285000, IP=0x80029 -- the rep movsb itself --
   then #DF. The handoff now builds its own 0-5 GiB identity map at 0x81000 and
   switches CR3 before the copy. Position-independent code still runs on a page
   table.

4. load_initrd() placed the initrd with `end = virt_to_phys(_start)`, under a
   comment reading "FILO itself is at the top of RAM. (relocated)" -- true on
   x86. Here _start is 0x100020, so `start = end - size` UNDERFLOWED to
   0xff501000 in a 512 MiB machine, and the `start < kern_end` guard passed
   with room to spare because a wrapped value is enormous. It now asks for the
   top of RAM directly (ram_top(), extracted so set_memory_size and this share
   one implementation) and checks for the overflow before the subtraction that
   used to wrap, not after.

5. struct e820entry declared `type` as `unsigned long`: 4 bytes on i386, 8 on
   LP64. So each entry was 24 bytes where the zero page says 20, and the kernel
   read entry 0 correctly (the low half of an 8-byte type is the right u32 on a
   little-endian machine) and garbage after. It concluded it had 640 KiB and
   panicked in init_mem_mapping -- BEFORE console_init, so the panic went to the
   printk ring buffer and never to a console:

       Kernel panic - not syncing: alloc_low_pages: can not alloc memory

   recovered by dumping 48 MiB of guest RAM over QMP. A correctly-running,
   completely silent kernel. Fixed-width types and packed, plus eight
   LINUX_ABI_ASSERT negative-array assertions on the offsets the zero page
   mandates -- because every field already carried its offset in a comment and
   comments do not fail a build. The control re-injects `unsigned long type`
   and the build stops on "size of array
   'linux_abi_e820entry_is_20_bytes' is negative".

Also adds the [IFDEF] CONFIG_AMD64 block forth/admin/nvram.fs never had, giving
amd64 load-base and little-endian?. x86's C-side `feval("%lx constant
load-base")` is deliberately NOT copied: it exists because relocation rebases
x86's GDT and a constant physical address is wrong there by construction.
arch/amd64/segment.c sets virt_offset = 0, so the plain int-config is already
right, and transcribing the workaround would have added a second definition to
fix a cause that does not exist here.

The handoff is its own routine rather than a switch_to(): arch/amd64's context
frame carries rsi -- exactly where the 64-bit boot protocol wants boot_params --
which makes the wrong answer look right. It carries no GDT and no segment
registers, on purpose, and the live long-mode GDT has 0x08 = code, 0x10 = data,
where the protocol requires CS = 0x10 and DS = 0x18. No context switch can fix
that.

--- a/config/examples/amd64_config.xml
+++ b/config/examples/amd64_config.xml
@@ -42,7 +42,7 @@
   <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_REISERFS" type="boolean" value="true"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_XFS" type="boolean" value="false"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_UFS" type="boolean" value="false"/>
-  <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_ISO9660" type="boolean" value="false"/>
+  <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_ISO9660" type="boolean" value="true"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_FFS" type="boolean" value="false"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_VSTAFS" type="boolean" value="false"/>
   <option name="CONFIG_FSYS_NTFS" type="boolean" value="false"/>
--- a/forth/admin/nvram.fs
+++ b/forth/admin/nvram.fs
@@ -364,6 +364,31 @@
 s" 4000000"  s" load-base"            int-config
 [THEN]
 
+[IFDEF] CONFIG_AMD64
+\ ---- AMD64 ----
+\ The per-arch section below had PPC, X86, SPARC32 and SPARC64 arms and no
+\ amd64 one (the only CONFIG_AMD64 test in this file was Spike 1's auto-boot?
+\ arm further up), so the 64-bit firmware got neither of these -- and
+\ `load-base` being undefined is not cosmetic: it is
+\ the SAME defect the x86 comment above describes, reached the same way.
+\ `boot <file>` that fails falls through to "Trying disk...", whose $load runs
+\ `" load-base" evaluate`, and an undefined word there ends the boot path in
+\ a Stack Underflow rather than an error anyone can read.
+\
+\ NOTE WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* COPIED FROM x86: arch/x86/openbios.c also
+\ feval()s a `constant load-base` over the top of this one, because x86
+\ relocates itself by rebasing the GDT and every Forth address is therefore
+\ segment-relative -- a constant PHYSICAL address is wrong there by
+\ construction. amd64 does not relocate (long mode ignores segment bases;
+\ arch/amd64/segment.c sets virt_offset = 0 and says so), so phys_to_virt()
+\ is the identity and this value is already correct. Transcribing x86's
+\ workaround would have added a second definition -- x86 prints "load-base
+\ isn't unique." on every boot because of it -- to fix a cause that does not
+\ exist here.
+s" true"     s" little-endian?"       bool-config
+s" 4000000"  s" load-base"            int-config
+[THEN]
+
 [IFDEF] CONFIG_SPARC32
 \ ---- SPARC32 ----
 s" 4000"     s" load-base"             int-config
